es — check whether any new error type was added or if `*ProcessError`'s shape is being relied upon implicitly.\n\n**Evaluate each new/modified type on:**\n\n- **Encapsulation**: Are fields exposed that should be private behind methods? For the new callback fields on `RunConfig`, is the call site free to mutate state through them in surprising ways?\n- **Invariant expression**: Does the type prevent invalid states? E.g. should `EnrichSummary` be a method on an interface (so a concrete enricher can have its own state), or is a bare function appropriate? Is a nil callback supported and documented? Is the contract \"must be pure / must return same-or-enriched / must not panic\" expressible in the type or only in prose?\n- **Usefulness**: Do the types pull their weight? Could simpler shapes (e.g. an interface with one method, or no abstraction at all) work?\n- **Enforcement**: Could a caller pass garbage that compiles but breaks at runtime? For the manifest, is there validation that catches malformed inputs at construction time vs. at write time?\n\n**Repo conventions (from CLAUDE.md):**\n- Don't introduce abstractions beyond what the task requires.\n- Don't design for hypothetical future requirements.\n- The review package has a documented architecture (`AgentReviewer` interface, `Sink` interface, `ReviewerTemplate` scaffolding). New types should fit, not duplicate.\n\nRate each new type on encapsulation, invariant expression, usefulness, enforcement (qualitative + brief quantitative scores). Be concrete about trade-offs. Cite file:line.","agentId":"aa848643d0b65f340","agentType":"pr-review-toolkit:type-design-analyzer","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I have sufficient information. Let me synthesize the analysis.\n\n---\n\n# Type Design Analysis — PR #1167\n\n## Scope correction\n\nThe user's brief describes `manifest.go` as a NEW file, but it pre-exists in `main` (introduced in 079fec2fa for `entire review --fix`). This PR appends ~130 lines of token-hydration helpers — no new exported types in that file. The only new exported surface added by the PR is two function-typed fields on the pre-existing `RunConfig`. `ProcessError` is also pre-existing (defined in `cmd/entire/cli/review/types/template.go:153`); the PR only adds new *consumers* (`dump.go`, `tui_model.go`) that use `errors.As` against it.\n\nSo this analysis really evaluates **two function-typed fields on `RunConfig`**, plus a brief check that `ProcessError`'s implicit contract holds up against its new consumers.\n\n---\n\n## Type: RunConfig (modified)\n\n**File:** `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/cmd/entire/cli/review/types/reviewer.go:105-114`\n\n```go\nEnrichSummary  func(context.Context, RunSummary) RunSummary\nEnrichAgentRun func(context.Context, AgentRun) AgentRun\n```\n\n### Invariants Identified\n\n- **Optional**: nil is a documented valid value; both `run.go:125` and `run_multi.go:238` nil-check before calling.\n- **Pure-by-convention**: callbacks return the (possibly enriched) value rather than mutate by reference, signalling functional semantics.\n- **Sequencing (Summary)**: `EnrichSummary` runs after the agent's stream closes but before `sink.RunFinished` (`run.go:117`, `run_multi.go:222`). Sinks therefore see the enriched view, never the raw view.\n- **Sequencing (AgentRun)**: `EnrichAgentRun` runs after that agent's `proc.Wait()` returns, on the per-agent forwarder goroutine, and only feeds back into the dispatch loop via a single `Tokens` event (`run_multi.go:240-244`). It is *not* used to rewrite `AgentRun` in the final summary — only to inject one fresh `Tokens` event.\n- **Concurrency**: in `RunMulti`, `EnrichAgentRun` is called from the per-agent forwarder goroutine, after the events channel drains but before `wg.Done`. The fan-in chan absorbs the synthetic event. Callers must therefore be goroutine-safe across N agents (the closure captures only `worktreeRoot`/`headSHA` strings, so the current call sites are safe).\n- **Token-only contract (de facto)**: `EnrichAgentRun`'s output is discarded except for `enriched.Tokens` (`run_multi.go:241-244`). Returning anything else (changed `Name`, `Buffer`, `Err`) is silently dropped.\n\n### Ratings\n\n- **Encapsulation: 5/10**\n  These are exported, mutable, function-valued fields on a struct that is otherwise treated as immutable per-run config. The doc comment (line 70) literally says \"RunConfig is the immutable per-run configuration\" — and the new fields break that promise by accepting closures that can do arbitrary I/O and mutation. The current call sites at `cmd.go:394` *do* mutate `runCfg.EnrichSummary` post-construction (`runCfg.EnrichSummary = reviewSummaryTokenEnricher(...)` after `applyReviewConfig(&runCfg, cfg)`), confirming this isn't a hypothetical.\n\n- **Invariant Expression: 4/10**\n  The \"this is really only used for token hydration\" contract isn't in the type — it's in the closure's discarded return value in `run_multi.go`. A caller writing `EnrichAgentRun = func(...) AgentRun { run.Err = nil; return run }` will compile cleanly, run, and have its `Err` change silently dropped. The function signature claims it can enrich the whole `AgentRun`; the implementation only honors `Tokens`. That's a Hyrum's-Law-grade trap.\n\n- **Invariant Usefulness: 6/10**\n  The underlying need is real: token totals are flushed by the agent's *lifecycle hook* after the event stream closes, and sinks need them at `RunFinished` time. A callback is one reasonable way to bridge that timing gap. But the callbacks are not pulling their weight as *generalised* enrichers — they are token hydrators with a generic name. The actual call sites (`reviewSummaryTokenEnricher`, `reviewAgentRunTokenEnricher` at `cmd.go:685-705`) wrap the same two `hydrateReview…` functions; nothing else uses these hooks today.\n\n- **Invariant Enforcement: 3/10**\n  Nothing prevents a callback from:\n  - panicking (no `defer recover` around the call — would kill the orchestrator goroutine, and in `run_multi.go` would leak the fan-in channel and other goroutines)\n  - blocking forever (no timeout; this is invoked on the dispatch path)\n  - mutating shared state through captured pointers\n  - violating the \"return same-or-enriched\" prose contract\n  - ignoring the passed `context.Context` (no cancellation check at the call site either — see `cmd.go:686-693`, which ignores `ctx.Done()` while doing file I/O via `os.ReadFile` and store ops)\n\n### Strengths\n\n- Optional (nil-safe), correctly documented as such.\n- Functional-style signature (value in, value out) is the right shape for an *enrichment* hook — better than a mutator like `func(*RunSummary)`.\n- The point of invocation is well-chosen: between event-stream close and `RunFinished` notification, where sinks have not yet been told the run is over.\n\n### Concerns\n\n1. **The name oversells the contract.** Both callbacks claim to enrich the whole shape but the multi-agent path silently discards everything except `Tokens` (`run_multi.go:241-244`). If you only care about tokens, the parameter shape should be `func(context.Context, AgentRun) Tokens` — that makes the actual contract type-level instead of a footgun.\n\n2. **`RunConfig`'s \"immutable per-run configuration\" promise is now false.** Line 70's doc comment needs updating, or — better — the fields belong on a separate \"hooks\" struct so the data half of `RunConfig` stays a value type and the behaviour half is clearly out-of-band.\n\n3. **No panic safety at the call site.** A poorly-written enricher takes down the orchestrator. Compare with `Sink`'s docs which spell out \"must not block, must not internally synchronize\" — `EnrichSummary` doesn't even have that level of prose.\n\n4. **Context handling is purely advisory.** The signature *takes* a context but the orchestrator doesn't check `ctx.Err()` before calling, and the production enricher at `cmd.go:686-693` does blocking disk I/O without honouring it.\n\n5. **Asymmetry with the rest of the package.** The review package already has well-designed interfaces (`AgentReviewer`, `Sink`, `Process`, `ReviewerTemplate`). Token hydration is the only behaviour injected as a bare `func` field on a config struct. A `SummaryEnricher` interface with a single method would slot in alongside `Sink` consistently — or, since the only real use is token hydration, the dependency could be a `TokenHydrator` interface (or even no abstraction at all — see below).\n\n### Recommended Improvements\n\nPick one of these in order of preference:\n\n**Option A (simplest, fits \"no abstractions beyond what the task requires\"):** delete the callbacks. Move token hydration into the orchestrator as an explicit step. `Run` and `RunMulti` already live in the same package as `hydrateReview…`. The reason for the callback indirection appears to be a (now violated) layering where `types/reviewer.go` is supposed to be agnostic of `session` — but the call already happens from `cmd/entire/cli/review/run.go`, which can import either form. A direct call removes the type-system gap entirely.\n\n**Option B (tighter type contract, smallest churn):** change the signatures to return only the value that's actually consumed:\n```go\nEnrichSummaryTokens  func(context.Context, RunSummary) RunSummary\nEnrichAgentRunTokens func(context.Context, AgentRun) Tokens\n```\nThe `AgentRun` form makes the \"only Tokens is used\" contract explicit. Rename `EnrichSummary` to `EnrichSummaryTokens` to set the same expectation.\n\n**Option C (most idiomatic for this package):** introduce a small interface alongside `Sink`:\n```go\ntype Enricher interface {\n    EnrichSummary(context.Context, RunSummary) RunSummary\n    EnrichAgentRun(context.Context, AgentRun) AgentRun\n}\n```\nPass it as a separate `Run`/`RunMulti` parameter (not a field on `RunConfig`), restoring `RunConfig`'s immutability promise. Allow a nil receiver to mean \"no enrichment\". This also lets a concrete enricher hold state (e.g. a `*session.StateStore` it lists once rather than per call).\n\n**Option D (orthogonal):** add a panic-recover guard at the two call sites (`run.go:127`, `run_multi.go:241`) and check `ctx.Err()` before invoking. Cheap, defensive, no API change.\n\n---\n\n## Type: LocalReviewManifest / ManifestSource (unchanged in PR)\n\nReviewing for completeness given the user's prompt asked, even though both types pre-exist:\n\n**File:** `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/cmd/entire/cli/review/manifest.go:25-42`\n\n### Ratings (existing design, not modified by this PR)\n\n- **Encapsulation: 4/10** — bare struct, all fields exported, JSON-tagged. Necessary for JSON marshalling, but there is no constructor enforcing invariants; `LocalReviewManifest{}` is a valid zero value that callers can pass around.\n- **Invariant Expression: 5/10** — `Version` is an `int` with no enum/const-binding to `localReviewManifestVersion`. `Status` is a free-form `string` (`session.AgentStatus.String()` output, but typed `string`). `Sources` is the only field with a non-trivial invariant (\"non-empty for a writable manifest\"), and that invariant is enforced only at write time (`writeLocalReviewManifest:271`: `if len(manifest.Sources) == 0 { return errors.New(...) }`), not at construction time.\n- **Invariant Usefulness: 7/10** — the manifest does pull its weight: it's the lookup index for `entire review --fix <session-id>`. Sharding by session id + timestamp filename is sensible.\n- **Invariant Enforcement: 5/10** — `writeLocalReviewManifest` (`manifest.go:271`) validates empty `Sources` and fills in zero-value `Version`/`CreatedAt`. But on the *read* path (`loadLocalReviewManifests:307`), there's no version compatibility check: a v2 manifest produced by a future build would silently decode into v1 fields. Adding `if manifest.Version != localReviewManifestVersion { /* skip with debug log */ }` would be a one-line improvement.\n\n### Concerns specific to the PR's additions to this file\n\nThe new `reviewAgentByType = agent.GetByAgentType` package-level var (`manifest.go:24`) is a test seam exposed as mutable global. The trailing comment (\"overridable in tests without mutating the global agent registry\") is honest about the trade-off but the pattern means production code shares a single function reference across the package. Acceptable for this scope; flag it as a smell if more such seams appear.\n\n---\n\n## Type: ProcessError (unchanged by PR; new consumers added)\n\n**File:** `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/cmd/entire/cli/review/types/template.go:153-166`\n\nNew consumers introduced by this PR:\n- `dump.go:107` (`errors.As(runErr, &pe)`)\n- `tui_model.go:441` (`errors.As(...)`)\n\n### Ratings (for the existing type, evaluated against the new dependencies)\n\n- **Encapsulation: 6/10** — exported fields (`AgentName`, `Err`, `Stderr`) are necessary for `errors.As`-based access. Acceptable.\n- **Invariant Expression: 7/10** — implements `Error()` and `Unwrap()`, so it composes correctly with `errors.Is`/`errors.As`. The doc comment at template.go:150 spells out the contract.\n- **Invariant Usefulness: 8/10** — captures the missing piece (agent stderr) that was the entire motivation for this PR's dump/TUI changes. The pre-existing design pays off here.\n- **Invariant Enforcement: 6/10** — there is no constructor; only `templateProcess.Wait` (`template.go:132`) builds one, and it does so only when `stderr != \"\"`. That implicit invariant (\"`Stderr` is non-empty when this type is constructed\") is *relied on* by `dump.go:107` (`if errors.As(...) && pe.Stderr != \"\"`) — but defensively re-checked there, which is the right call given there's no constructor enforcing it.\n\n### Concerns\n\n- The PR's `sameFailureError` helper (`dump.go:117-122`) uses `errors.Is(a, b) || errors.Is(b, a)`. For `*ProcessError`, `errors.Is` falls back to identity comparison (no custom `Is`), which works *only* because `run.Err` and the synthetic `RunError.Err` are the literal same value at `run.go:84-92` and `run_multi.go:131-134`. This identity coupling is implicit and one refactor away from breaking (e.g. wrapping the wait error at one site but not the other). A `func (e *ProcessError) Is(target error) bool` implementation, or a sentinel \"synthetic from run.Err\" marker, would make this robust. Today: works, but fragile.\n\n---\n\n## Summary Table\n\n| Type | Encapsulation | Invariant Expression | Usefulness | Enforcement |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `RunConfig.EnrichSummary` / `EnrichAgentRun` (new) | 5/10 | 4/10 | 6/10 | 3/10 |\n| `LocalReviewManifest` (existing, helpers added) | 4/10 | 5/10 | 7/10 | 5/10 |\n| `ProcessError` (existing, new consumers) | 6/10 | 7/10 | 8/10 | 6/10 |\n\n## Top recommendation\n\nThe only PR-introduced API surface — the two function-typed fields on `RunConfig` — is the weakest of the lot. Prefer **Option A or B above**: either inline the hydration call into the orchestrator (zero abstraction, since both callers and helpers already live in the same package) or tighten the signatures so the type expresses the \"only `Tokens` is honoured\" contract instead of leaving it implicit. Option D (panic recovery + ctx check at the two call sites) is worth doing regardless of which structural option is picked — it's two small defensive edits and costs nothing.\n\nRelevant files:\n- `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/cmd/entire/cli/review/types/reviewer.go`\n- `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/cmd/entire/cli/review/run.go`\n- `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/cmd/entire/cli/review/run_multi.go`\n- `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/cmd/entire/cli/review/cmd.go`\n- `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/cmd/entire/cli/review/manifest.go`\n- `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/cmd/entire/cli/review/dump.go`\n- `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/cmd/entire/cli/review/types/template.go`"}],"totalDurationMs":154125,"totalTokens":75298,"totalToolUseCount":30,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":972,"cache_read_input_tokens":68761,"output_tokens":5564,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":972},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":5564,"cache_read_input_tokens":68761,"cache_creation_input_tokens":972,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":972,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":0,"searchCount":0,"bashCount":30,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":0}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"0e7a07a1-5093-4177-b5fd-a00240cb2854","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli","sessionId":"e755fa5c-b328-4df6-9d0e-c68b395e061e","version":"2.1.139","gitBranch":"feat/review-manifest-diagnostic"}
