{"parentUuid":"c39b0ed6-0d28-4529-a6ac-30e83b4c7ab5","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"a9614f47-41df-47ed-8947-b9c54a028dad","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RUVf6Ta5WYb9vZTHg1N8fq","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have what I need. Let me finalize my analysis.\n\n# Error Handling Audit: PR #1167 — Surface Agent Failures in Live Dashboard and Post-Run Dump\n\nI read the diff, the post-change files via `git show`, and the surrounding types. Overall this PR is doing the right thing for the headline failure paths — `shouldEmitSyntheticRunError`, the `ProcessError`-aware preview, and the `writeFailureHeader` stderr code-fence are all solid improvements. But there are several remaining silent-failure paths that work against the PR's stated goal of \"surface failures.\"\n\n---\n\n## Findings\n\n### 1. HIGH — Token-enrichment failures are logged at Debug, even when the operation that produced them is broken\n\n**Location:** `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/cmd/entire/cli/review/cmd.go:685-705` (PR branch).\n\n```go\nfunc reviewSummaryTokenEnricher(worktreeRoot, headSHA string) func(...) ... {\n    return func(ctx context.Context, summary reviewtypes.RunSummary) reviewtypes.RunSummary {\n        enriched, err := hydrateReviewSummaryTokensFromCurrentState(ctx, worktreeRoot, headSHA, summary)\n        if err != nil {\n            logging.Debug(ctx, \"review token hydration skipped\", slog.String(\"error\", err.Error()))\n            return summary\n        }\n        return enriched\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThe errors returned by `hydrateReviewSummaryTokensFromCurrentState` / `hydrateReviewAgentRunTokensFromCurrentState` come from `session.NewStateStore` and `store.List` (`manifest.go:97-106` and `:114-123`). Those failures mean **the session state store is broken** — `.git/entire-sessions/` is unreadable, the worktree root cannot be resolved, the git common dir lookup failed, etc. Those are the same failure modes that will also break `writePostReviewManifest` two lines later (which DOES surface a user-visible \"manifest not written\" note via `warnManifestNotWritten`).\n\nThe user-visible symptom is asymmetric:\n- `writePostReviewManifest` failure: visible warning + Debug log.\n- Token-enrichment failure: Debug log only; the row just renders with empty `TOKENS`.\n\nA user staring at a token column of zeros has no signal that anything went wrong — they'll assume the agent didn't emit token data. They cannot act on this without re-running with `ENTIRE_LOG_LEVEL=debug`.\n\n**Recommendation:** Either raise the severity to `Warn` (these errors are not routine — they indicate something is wrong with the state store the enricher needs), or piggyback on the existing manifest warning path. A minimal fix:\n\n```go\nif err != nil {\n    logging.Warn(ctx, \"review token hydration skipped\", slog.String(\"error\", err.Error()))\n    return summary\n}\n```\n\nIf a user-facing note is desired (preferable, given this PR's anti-silent-failure goal), the enricher should accept the user-output writer and print a one-liner like `Note: token totals unavailable (session state load failed).`\n\n---\n\n### 2. HIGH — `reviewTokenUsageForSession` silently returns nil on three distinct error paths\n\n**Location:** `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/cmd/entire/cli/review/manifest.go:171-190` (PR branch).\n\n```go\nfunc reviewTokenUsageForSession(ctx context.Context, st *session.State) *agent.TokenUsage {\n    if st == nil {\n        return nil\n    }\n    if hasReviewTokenUsageData(st.TokenUsage) {\n        return st.TokenUsage\n    }\n    if st.TranscriptPath == \"\" || st.AgentType == \"\" {\n        return nil\n    }\n    ag, err := reviewAgentByType(st.AgentType)\n    if err != nil {\n        return nil           // (a) agent registry lookup failure swallowed\n    }\n    transcript, err := os.ReadFile(st.TranscriptPath)\n    if err != nil {\n        return nil           // (b) transcript read failure swallowed\n    }\n    return agent.CalculateTokenUsage(ctx, ag, transcript, st.CheckpointTranscriptStart, reviewSubagentsDir(st))\n}\n```\n\nThree silent returns:\n- **(a)** Unknown agent type. This indicates a session state file references an agent that's no longer in the registry — a real configuration / migration bug worth surfacing.\n- **(b)** Transcript missing or unreadable. Permissions issue, the file was rotated, or the path is stale. Worth knowing about.\n- The third call (`agent.CalculateTokenUsage`) returns its own value without error — but if that ever changes to return an error, the current code silently discards it.\n\nThese are exactly the \"user gets zero tokens, no idea why\" failures. The enclosing `hydrateReviewSummaryTokensFromStates` and `hydrateReviewAgentRunTokensFromStates` cannot distinguish \"no token data\" from \"we couldn't compute token data\" because the helper returns the same `nil` in both cases.\n\n**Recommendation:** Return `(*agent.TokenUsage, error)` and let the caller log the distinct failure modes at Warn:\n\n```go\nfunc reviewTokenUsageForSession(ctx context.Context, st *session.State) (*agent.TokenUsage, error) {\n    // ...\n    ag, err := reviewAgentByType(st.AgentType)\n    if err != nil {\n        return nil, fmt.Errorf(\"resolve agent for token usage: %w\", err)\n    }\n    transcript, err := os.ReadFile(st.TranscriptPath)\n    if err != nil {\n        return nil, fmt.Errorf(\"read transcript for token usage: %w\", err)\n    }\n    return agent.CalculateTokenUsage(...), nil\n}\n```\n\nThen `hydrate*` should log the warning with the session ID for context, instead of swallowing.\n\n---\n\n### 3. HIGH — `os.WriteFile` for manifest is atomic-but-not-fsync; partial writes on power loss leave corrupt JSON readable\n\n**Location:** `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/cmd/entire/cli/review/manifest.go:281-309` (PR branch, `writeLocalReviewManifest`).\n\nThe write path is:\n```go\nif err := os.MkdirAll(dir, 0o700); err != nil { ... }\nb, err := json.MarshalIndent(manifest, \"\", \"  \")\nif err != nil { ... }\npath := filepath.Join(dir, localReviewManifestFilename(manifest))\nif err := os.WriteFile(path, b, 0o600); err != nil { ... }\n```\n\nThis is **not** a silent failure of error handling — the errors all propagate. But there's a latent silent-corruption hazard: a crash partway through `WriteFile` leaves a partial JSON file that `loadLocalReviewManifests` will then surface as `decode review manifest <name>: ...` — propagating, but only at the next `entire review --findings` / `--fix` invocation, with no remediation path described.\n\n**Recommendation:** Use the write-temp-then-rename pattern so partial writes cannot be observed:\n```go\ntmp := path + \".tmp\"\nif err := os.WriteFile(tmp, b, 0o600); err != nil { ... }\nif err := os.Rename(tmp, path); err != nil { ... }\n```\n\nThis is the standard fix for \"atomic-write a metadata file\" and matches what the strategy package does for session state.\n\n---\n\n### 4. MEDIUM — `enrichRunSummary` swallows panics from user-supplied enrichers\n\n**Location:** `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/cmd/entire/cli/review/run.go:119-124` and `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/cmd/entire/cli/review/run_multi.go:213` (`enrichRunSummary` + `emitEnrichedAgentTokens`).\n\nBoth `EnrichSummary` and `EnrichAgentRun` are arbitrary callbacks from `RunConfig`. A panic in the production enricher (`reviewSummaryTokenEnricher`) crashes the entire review run after the agent has already done its work — losing the summary, the dump, and the manifest. Because `emitEnrichedAgentTokens` runs inside a goroutine in `run_multi.go:127-144`, a panic there crashes the whole process.\n\nThere's no `recover()` on the enricher invocation paths. The enrichers themselves do error-handling, but the contract is implicit — a future enricher (or a deeper bug in `agent.CalculateTokenUsage`) that panics will bring down all sinks.\n\n**Recommendation:** Wrap enricher calls with `defer recover()` and treat a panic the same as an error — log at Warn with the enricher name, return the original summary/run unchanged. Example:\n\n```go\nfunc enrichRunSummary(ctx context.Context, cfg reviewtypes.RunConfig, summary reviewtypes.RunSummary) (out reviewtypes.RunSummary) {\n    if cfg.EnrichSummary == nil {\n        return summary\n    }\n    defer func() {\n        if r := recover(); r != nil {\n            logging.Warn(ctx, \"review enrich summary panicked\", slog.Any(\"panic\", r))\n            out = summary\n        }\n    }()\n    return cfg.EnrichSummary(ctx, summary)\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n### 5. MEDIUM — Synthetic-RunError cancellation guard can miss SIGTERM and `*exec.ExitError` with `-1` exit code\n\n**Location:** `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/cmd/entire/cli/review/run.go:135-145` (PR branch, `shouldEmitSyntheticRunError`).\n\n```go\nfunc shouldEmitSyntheticRunError(ctx context.Context, waitErr error) bool {\n    if waitErr == nil { return false }\n    if ctx.Err() != nil { return false }\n    if errors.Is(waitErr, context.Canceled) || errors.Is(waitErr, context.DeadlineExceeded) {\n        return false\n    }\n    return true\n}\n```\n\nThe guard only suppresses synthetic-RunError emission when:\n- `ctx.Err()` is non-nil, **or**\n- `waitErr` chains through `context.Canceled` / `context.DeadlineExceeded`.\n\nBut `templateProcess.Wait()` in `types/template.go:122-136` converts cancellation to `ctx.Err()` only when both `cmd.Wait()` returned an error AND `p.ctx.Err()` is non-nil. There's a window where:\n- A process receives SIGTERM (not from our cancel) — e.g., the user kills the agent's child directly, or the OS OOM-killer.\n- `cmd.Wait()` returns `*exec.ExitError` with `Signaled() == true`.\n- The orchestrator context was NOT cancelled.\n\nThis produces a \"real\" failure event, which is arguably correct — but the test `TestRunMulti_DoesNotEmitSyntheticRunErrorOnCancellation` (run_multi_test.go around line 794-820 in the PR) only exercises the `context.Canceled` waitErr path. It does not exercise the case where the parser already emitted a `Finished{Success:true}` event and the process then exits with `-1` due to a stray signal — that's the \"torn stdout\" scenario where you'd want both the in-stream Finished AND the synthetic RunError to surface, but the user gets confused output (\"succeeded\" + \"synthetic error\" together).\n\nThe classification in `classifyStatus` correctly demotes to `Failed` in this case (`waitErr != nil` after the `ctx.Err()` check). The concern is the user-visible double-message in the dump. Not a silent failure — but inverse of one: a noisy false-positive.\n\n**Recommendation:** Document the SIGTERM-from-outside-our-context case explicitly in `shouldEmitSyntheticRunError`. Optionally, when `errors.As(waitErr, &exitErr)` and `exitErr.ExitCode() == -1` AND no signal handler is configured, prefer the original waitErr without the synthetic event (it's already classified Failed; the synthetic event adds no information).\n\n---\n\n### 6. MEDIUM — `formatErrorPreview` returns the first non-empty stderr line; pure-ANSI stderr produces an empty preview that masks the failure\n\n**Location:** `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/cmd/entire/cli/review/tui_model.go:430-446` (PR branch, `formatErrorPreview`).\n\n```go\nfunc formatErrorPreview(err error) string {\n    if err == nil { return \"\" }\n    var pe *reviewtypes.ProcessError\n    if errors.As(err, &pe) {\n        for _, line := range strings.Split(pe.Stderr, \"\\n\") {\n            if trimmed := strings.TrimSpace(line); trimmed != \"\" {\n                return trimmed\n            }\n        }\n        if pe.Err != nil {\n            return pe.Err.Error()\n        }\n    }\n    return err.Error()\n}\n```\n\nThe \"first non-empty line\" rule is reasonable for plain text but breaks for stderr that contains only ANSI escape codes (think `codex` and `claude-code` emitting colored failure banners). After `strings.TrimSpace`, an ANSI-only line is non-empty (escape bytes are not whitespace), so the preview shows `\\x1b[31mError\\x1b[0m...` literally — which then runs through `sanitizeDisplayText` + `CollapseWhitespace` at the caller and may collapse to something user-hostile.\n\nMore subtly: if the first line is ANSI-only chrome and the second line is the real error message, the user sees the chrome and not the message.\n\n**Recommendation:** Strip ANSI escapes before picking the first non-empty line:\n```go\nline = stripAnsi(strings.TrimSpace(line))\nif line != \"\" { return line }\n```\n\nThere's already an `ansi` package in use (test file imports `ansi.StringWidth`). Use its strip helper, or `regexp.MustCompile(\"\\x1b\\\\[[0-9;]*m\").ReplaceAllString(line, \"\")`.\n\n**Also:** when `pe.Stderr` is entirely empty AND `pe.Err` is nil (defensive: both fields unset), the function falls through to `err.Error()` — which on a `*ProcessError` produces `\"<agent>: <nil>\"`. That's user-hostile but technically not silent. Worth adding `return \"(no error detail available)\"` as a final fallback.\n\n---\n\n### 7. MEDIUM — `dumpAgent` swallows `mdrender.RenderForWriter` errors twice — once silently per file\n\n**Location:** `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/cmd/entire/cli/review/dump.go:90-95` (current; predates this PR but the PR builds on top of it without addressing).\n\n```go\nrendered, err := mdrender.RenderForWriter(s.W, b.String())\nif err != nil {\n    rendered = b.String()\n}\nfmt.Fprint(s.W, rendered)\n```\n\nThis is a fallback that's reasonable in spirit but doesn't log. If the markdown renderer is broken (a real regression in `mdrender`), every user gets the raw markdown back with no indication something failed. Since this PR doubles down on the dump path as the primary failure-visibility surface, a `logging.Debug` here would help operators triage \"renderer broken\" reports.\n\n**Recommendation:** Add a `logging.Debug` when the fallback fires. Not a Warn — the user still sees content, so this is genuinely a soft fallback.\n\n---\n\n### 8. LOW — `fanIn` channel send in `emitEnrichedAgentTokens` can block indefinitely if the dispatch loop has already exited\n\n**Location:** `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/cmd/entire/cli/review/run_multi.go:213` (PR branch).\n\n```go\nfanIn <- taggedEvent{agentIdx: agentIdx, ev: enriched.Tokens}\n```\n\nThis send happens inside the per-agent forwarding goroutine **after** `p.Wait()` returns. The `wg.Wait()`/`close(fanIn)` orchestration runs in a separate goroutine and is only triggered by `wg.Done`, which only fires when the forwarding goroutine returns. So the send is safe — but a future change that returns early from the forwarding goroutine (e.g., a `select` with `ctx.Done()`) would deadlock here because `close(fanIn)` doesn't happen until `wg.Done()`.\n\nThis is not a current bug, but it's a fragile invariant worth documenting. The `fanIn` channel has buffer `len(reviewers)*16`, but for N=8 agents emitting Started + AssistantText × 10 + Tokens + Finished + synthetic RunError + enriched Tokens, you can easily exceed 128 buffered events. Synchronous blocking sends then depend on the dispatch loop draining at line `for tagged := range fanIn` — which is fine in the normal path.\n\n**Recommendation:** Document the invariant in the function-level comment, or wrap the send with a `select { case fanIn <- ...: case <-ctx.Done(): }` to drop the enrichment on cancellation. Right now if `ctx` is cancelled mid-run, the enrichment can still be queued (and consumed) which is functional but wasteful.\n\n---\n\n### 9. LOW — `enriched` `AgentRun` is constructed but only `Tokens` is read; other fields silently discarded\n\n**Location:** `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/cmd/entire/cli/review/run_multi.go:208-214` (PR branch, `emitEnrichedAgentTokens`).\n\n```go\nenriched := cfg.EnrichAgentRun(ctx, run)\nif enriched.Tokens.In == 0 && enriched.Tokens.Out == 0 {\n    return\n}\nfanIn <- taggedEvent{agentIdx: agentIdx, ev: enriched.Tokens}\n```\n\nThe enricher gets the full `AgentRun` and can return a modified `AgentRun`. But the dispatch loop only consumes the `enriched.Tokens` field via the synthetic `Tokens` event. If an enricher returns `Err` set to something non-nil (e.g., \"transcript truncated; tokens are partial\"), that error is silently dropped. The function name implies enrichment, but the contract is hidden: only `Tokens` enrichment actually flows.\n\n**Recommendation:** Either:\n- Document this restriction on `RunConfig.EnrichAgentRun`: \"only `Tokens` modifications are propagated to live sinks; other field changes are ignored until the summary-level `EnrichSummary` runs.\"\n- Or fan out a richer event type (e.g., a hypothetical `AgentRunUpdate` event) and consume the whole struct.\n\nThe current behavior is fine for the immediate use case but invites future bugs where a maintainer wires up additional fields and wonders why they don't appear.\n\n---\n\n### 10. INFO — `tui_sink.go:83-89` swallows `program.Run()` error with explicit `_ = err`\n\n**Location:** `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/cmd/entire/cli/review/tui_sink.go:81-90`.\n\n```go\ngo func() {\n    defer close(s.done)\n    if _, err := s.program.Run(); err != nil {\n        // Bubble Tea program errors are non-actionable in a background\n        // goroutine (e.g., terminal resize race on exit). Log nothing —\n        // the run result is available via RunSummary and the sink's\n        // finished state. Swallowing is intentional.\n        _ = err\n    }\n}()\n```\n\nThis was **not changed by the PR** but is right next to changes that this PR introduces (terminal measurer). The comment justifies the swallow (race on exit), but the same `program.Run()` can fail for legitimate reasons: terminal init failure (no /dev/tty access despite the TTY check), bubbletea panic recovered into an error, alt-screen entry failure. None of those would surface.\n\nGiven this PR's explicit goal is to surface failures, a one-line `logging.Debug(context.Background(), \"review tui program exited\", slog.String(\"error\", err.Error()))` would be cheap and let operators triage \"the TUI never appeared\" reports.\n\n**Recommendation:** Add a Debug log. Don't elevate to Warn (the comment is right that this is often non-actionable) — but no-log makes the failure mode invisible.\n\n---\n\n### Not concerning (verified)\n\n- `dump.go:74-95` `writeFailureHeader` — handles all three branches (nil error, `*ProcessError` with stderr, generic error). Good.\n- `dump.go:91-95` empty-stderr branch — when `pe.Stderr == \"\"`, falls through to the generic `**Failed:** \\`<err>\\`` format. Correct.\n- `dump.go:78-86` `sameFailureError` deduping — uses `errors.Is(a, b) || errors.Is(b, a)`. Symmetric.\n- `run.go:90-99` synthetic RunError emission — appends to buffer AND fans out to sinks live. Good.\n- `tui_model.go:122-141` `runFinishedMsg` handling — preserves event-stream-set state (RunError stickiness) and only fills gaps. Correct.\n- `tui_model.go:145-158` `tickMsg` / `spinner.TickMsg` finished-guard — prevents post-finish spinner redraws. Good.\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\nThe PR ships its headline goal correctly: synthetic RunError emission, `*ProcessError` stderr in the dump, and dashboard error previews all work. The remaining silent-failure paths are concentrated in the **token-enrichment pipeline**, which is a new addition to this PR and inherits the \"log Debug + return original\" anti-pattern in three places (findings #1, #2, possibly #4). Those should be Warn-level or visible to the user, because the surrounding manifest path already commits to user-visible \"something didn't persist\" notes — token enrichment failing for the same reason should not be uniquely silent.\n\nThe most actionable fixes, in order:\n1. **#1**: Promote `logging.Debug` to `logging.Warn` for both enricher closures in `cmd.go:685-705`.\n2. **#2**: Return errors from `reviewTokenUsageForSession` (`manifest.go:171-190`) and log them at Warn.\n3. **#4**: Add `recover()` to `enrichRunSummary` and `emitEnrichedAgentTokens` — these are untrusted callback boundaries.\n4. **#6**: Strip ANSI before picking the first non-empty stderr line in `formatErrorPreview` (`tui_model.go:430-446`).\n5. **#3**: Use atomic temp-then-rename in `writeLocalReviewManifest` (`manifest.go:281-309`).\n\n**Files referenced (absolute):**\n- `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/cmd/entire/cli/review/cmd.go`\n- `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/cmd/entire/cli/review/manifest.go`\n- `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/cmd/entire/cli/review/run.go`\n- `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/cmd/entire/cli/review/run_multi.go`\n- `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/cmd/entire/cli/review/dump.go`\n- `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/cmd/entire/cli/review/tui_model.go`\n- `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/cmd/entire/cli/review/tui_sink.go`\n- `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/cmd/entire/cli/review/types/template.go` (for `ProcessError` shape)"},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a58c1a15810e56e18 (use SendMessage with to: 'a58c1a15810e56e18' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 122649\ntool_uses: 32\nduration_ms: 199910</usage>"}]}]},"uuid":"91dd17b6-c797-49bb-8620-0c3a8b1b3383","timestamp":"2026-05-12T03:57:19.766Z","toolUseResult":{"status":"completed","prompt":"Hunt for silent failures and inadequate error handling in PR #1167 of entireio/cli. The PR is titled \"feat(review): surface agent failures in live dashboard and post-run dump\" — it explicitly aims to SURFACE failures rather than swallow them, so any remaining silent-failure paths are particularly relevant.\n\n**How to see the changes:**\n- Diff at `/tmp/pr-1167.diff` (1421 lines).\n- Post-change files via `git show pr-1167-review:<path>`.\n- Do NOT check out the branch — work from the diff and `git show`.\n\n**Files to scrutinize (focus on production code, but check tests for ignored errors too):**\n- cmd/entire/cli/review/cmd.go — new enricher closures around `hydrateReviewSummaryTokensFromCurrentState` / `hydrateReviewAgentRunTokensFromCurrentState` use `logging.Debug` on error and return original summary/run. Is \"debug log + return original\" the right severity for this kind of failure, or should it be Warn? Does it mask real bugs?\n- cmd/entire/cli/review/run.go — synthetic `RunError` emission path on `proc.Wait()` failure. Cancellation guard: when do we skip emission? Could a genuine failure ever be misclassified as cancellation (e.g. SIGTERM converted to context error)?\n- cmd/entire/cli/review/run_multi.go — same pattern multi-agent.\n- cmd/entire/cli/review/dump.go — failed-row preview extraction from `*ProcessError`; what happens when stderr is empty, when the error isn't a ProcessError, when it contains only ANSI escapes?\n- cmd/entire/cli/review/manifest.go — NEW file, manifest write path: errors from filesystem writes, permission failures, partial writes — are they propagated, logged, or silently dropped? Does the PR include a `warnManifestNotWritten` path; under what conditions does it fire vs. silently no-op?\n- cmd/entire/cli/review/tui_model.go / tui_sink.go — bubbletea errors, alt-screen entry failures, terminal width measurement fallback when no TTY.\n\n**Red flags to look for specifically:**\n- `_ = err` or naked `if err != nil { return }` without any user signal\n- `logging.Debug` for things that should be `Warn` or `Error` (user-actionable failures)\n- catch-and-return-zero-value patterns (e.g. enricher returning original on error — is this a fallback or a swallow?)\n- Error returned to a goroutine with no consumer\n- `*ProcessError` stderr being trimmed/filtered in a way that could empty out genuine signal\n\n**Repo conventions:**\n- Internal logging uses `logging.Debug/Info/Warn/Error(ctx, msg, attrs...)`.\n- User-facing output goes through `cmd.OutOrStdout()` / `cmd.ErrOrStderr()`.\n- The review package's whole point is making errors visible; silent failure is squarely an anti-goal here.\n\nReport any silent failures or inadequate error handling with file:line refs and concrete fixes.","agentId":"a58c1a15810e56e18","agentType":"pr-review-toolkit:silent-failure-hunter","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have what I need. Let me finalize my analysis.\n\n# Error Handling Audit: PR #1167 — Surface Agent Failures in Live Dashboard and Post-Run Dump\n\nI read the diff, the post-change files via `git show`, and the surrounding types. Overall this PR is doing the right thing for the headline failure paths — `shouldEmitSyntheticRunError`, the `ProcessError`-aware preview, and the `writeFailureHeader` stderr code-fence are all solid improvements. But there are several remaining silent-failure paths that work against the PR's stated goal of \"surface failures.\"\n\n---\n\n## Findings\n\n### 1. HIGH — Token-enrichment failures are logged at Debug, even when the operation that produced them is broken\n\n**Location:** `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/cmd/entire/cli/review/cmd.go:685-705` (PR branch).\n\n```go\nfunc reviewSummaryTokenEnricher(worktreeRoot, headSHA string) func(...) ... {\n    return func(ctx context.Context, summary reviewtypes.RunSummary) reviewtypes.RunSummary {\n        enriched, err := hydrateReviewSummaryTokensFromCurrentState(ctx, worktreeRoot, headSHA, summary)\n        if err != nil {\n            logging.Debug(ctx, \"review token hydration skipped\", slog.String(\"error\", err.Error()))\n            return summary\n        }\n        return enriched\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThe errors returned by `hydrateReviewSummaryTokensFromCurrentState` / `hydrateReviewAgentRunTokensFromCurrentState` come from `session.NewStateStore` and `store.List` (`manifest.go:97-106` and `:114-123`). Those failures mean **the session state store is broken** — `.git/entire-sessions/` is unreadable, the worktree root cannot be resolved, the git common dir lookup failed, etc. Those are the same failure modes that will also break `writePostReviewManifest` two lines later (which DOES surface a user-visible \"manifest not written\" note via `warnManifestNotWritten`).\n\nThe user-visible symptom is asymmetric:\n- `writePostReviewManifest` failure: visible warning + Debug log.\n- Token-enrichment failure: Debug log only; the row just renders with empty `TOKENS`.\n\nA user staring at a token column of zeros has no signal that anything went wrong — they'll assume the agent didn't emit token data. They cannot act on this without re-running with `ENTIRE_LOG_LEVEL=debug`.\n\n**Recommendation:** Either raise the severity to `Warn` (these errors are not routine — they indicate something is wrong with the state store the enricher needs), or piggyback on the existing manifest warning path. A minimal fix:\n\n```go\nif err != nil {\n    logging.Warn(ctx, \"review token hydration skipped\", slog.String(\"error\", err.Error()))\n    return summary\n}\n```\n\nIf a user-facing note is desired (preferable, given this PR's anti-silent-failure goal), the enricher should accept the user-output writer and print a one-liner like `Note: token totals unavailable (session state load failed).`\n\n---\n\n### 2. HIGH — `reviewTokenUsageForSession` silently returns nil on three distinct error paths\n\n**Location:** `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/cmd/entire/cli/review/manifest.go:171-190` (PR branch).\n\n```go\nfunc reviewTokenUsageForSession(ctx context.Context, st *session.State) *agent.TokenUsage {\n    if st == nil {\n        return nil\n    }\n    if hasReviewTokenUsageData(st.TokenUsage) {\n        return st.TokenUsage\n    }\n    if st.TranscriptPath == \"\" || st.AgentType == \"\" {\n        return nil\n    }\n    ag, err := reviewAgentByType(st.AgentType)\n    if err != nil {\n        return nil           // (a) agent registry lookup failure swallowed\n    }\n    transcript, err := os.ReadFile(st.TranscriptPath)\n    if err != nil {\n        return nil           // (b) transcript read failure swallowed\n    }\n    return agent.CalculateTokenUsage(ctx, ag, transcript, st.CheckpointTranscriptStart, reviewSubagentsDir(st))\n}\n```\n\nThree silent returns:\n- **(a)** Unknown agent type. This indicates a session state file references an agent that's no longer in the registry — a real configuration / migration bug worth surfacing.\n- **(b)** Transcript missing or unreadable. Permissions issue, the file was rotated, or the path is stale. Worth knowing about.\n- The third call (`agent.CalculateTokenUsage`) returns its own value without error — but if that ever changes to return an error, the current code silently discards it.\n\nThese are exactly the \"user gets zero tokens, no idea why\" failures. The enclosing `hydrateReviewSummaryTokensFromStates` and `hydrateReviewAgentRunTokensFromStates` cannot distinguish \"no token data\" from \"we couldn't compute token data\" because the helper returns the same `nil` in both cases.\n\n**Recommendation:** Return `(*agent.TokenUsage, error)` and let the caller log the distinct failure modes at Warn:\n\n```go\nfunc reviewTokenUsageForSession(ctx context.Context, st *session.State) (*agent.TokenUsage, error) {\n    // ...\n    ag, err := reviewAgentByType(st.AgentType)\n    if err != nil {\n        return nil, fmt.Errorf(\"resolve agent for token usage: %w\", err)\n    }\n    transcript, err := os.ReadFile(st.TranscriptPath)\n    if err != nil {\n        return nil, fmt.Errorf(\"read transcript for token usage: %w\", err)\n    }\n    return agent.CalculateTokenUsage(...), nil\n}\n```\n\nThen `hydrate*` should log the warning with the session ID for context, instead of swallowing.\n\n---\n\n### 3. HIGH — `os.WriteFile` for manifest is atomic-but-not-fsync; partial writes on power loss leave corrupt JSON readable\n\n**Location:** `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/cmd/entire/cli/review/manifest.go:281-309` (PR branch, `writeLocalReviewManifest`).\n\nThe write path is:\n```go\nif err := os.MkdirAll(dir, 0o700); err != nil { ... }\nb, err := json.MarshalIndent(manifest, \"\", \"  \")\nif err != nil { ... }\npath := filepath.Join(dir, localReviewManifestFilename(manifest))\nif err := os.WriteFile(path, b, 0o600); err != nil { ... }\n```\n\nThis is **not** a silent failure of error handling — the errors all propagate. But there's a latent silent-corruption hazard: a crash partway through `WriteFile` leaves a partial JSON file that `loadLocalReviewManifests` will then surface as `decode review manifest <name>: ...` — propagating, but only at the next `entire review --findings` / `--fix` invocation, with no remediation path described.\n\n**Recommendation:** Use the write-temp-then-rename pattern so partial writes cannot be observed:\n```go\ntmp := path + \".tmp\"\nif err := os.WriteFile(tmp, b, 0o600); err != nil { ... }\nif err := os.Rename(tmp, path); err != nil { ... }\n```\n\nThis is the standard fix for \"atomic-write a metadata file\" and matches what the strategy package does for session state.\n\n---\n\n### 4. MEDIUM — `enrichRunSummary` swallows panics from user-supplied enrichers\n\n**Location:** `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/cmd/entire/cli/review/run.go:119-124` and `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/cmd/entire/cli/review/run_multi.go:213` (`enrichRunSummary` + `emitEnrichedAgentTokens`).\n\nBoth `EnrichSummary` and `EnrichAgentRun` are arbitrary callbacks from `RunConfig`. A panic in the production enricher (`reviewSummaryTokenEnricher`) crashes the entire review run after the agent has already done its work — losing the summary, the dump, and the manifest. Because `emitEnrichedAgentTokens` runs inside a goroutine in `run_multi.go:127-144`, a panic there crashes the whole process.\n\nThere's no `recover()` on the enricher invocation paths. The enrichers themselves do error-handling, but the contract is implicit — a future enricher (or a deeper bug in `agent.CalculateTokenUsage`) that panics will bring down all sinks.\n\n**Recommendation:** Wrap enricher calls with `defer recover()` and treat a panic the same as an error — log at Warn with the enricher name, return the original summary/run unchanged. Example:\n\n```go\nfunc enrichRunSummary(ctx context.Context, cfg reviewtypes.RunConfig, summary reviewtypes.RunSummary) (out reviewtypes.RunSummary) {\n    if cfg.EnrichSummary == nil {\n        return summary\n    }\n    defer func() {\n        if r := recover(); r != nil {\n            logging.Warn(ctx, \"review enrich summary panicked\", slog.Any(\"panic\", r))\n            out = summary\n        }\n    }()\n    return cfg.EnrichSummary(ctx, summary)\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n### 5. MEDIUM — Synthetic-RunError cancellation guard can miss SIGTERM and `*exec.ExitError` with `-1` exit code\n\n**Location:** `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/cmd/entire/cli/review/run.go:135-145` (PR branch, `shouldEmitSyntheticRunError`).\n\n```go\nfunc shouldEmitSyntheticRunError(ctx context.Context, waitErr error) bool {\n    if waitErr == nil { return false }\n    if ctx.Err() != nil { return false }\n    if errors.Is(waitErr, context.Canceled) || errors.Is(waitErr, context.DeadlineExceeded) {\n        return false\n    }\n    return true\n}\n```\n\nThe guard only suppresses synthetic-RunError emission when:\n- `ctx.Err()` is non-nil, **or**\n- `waitErr` chains through `context.Canceled` / `context.DeadlineExceeded`.\n\nBut `templateProcess.Wait()` in `types/template.go:122-136` converts cancellation to `ctx.Err()` only when both `cmd.Wait()` returned an error AND `p.ctx.Err()` is non-nil. There's a window where:\n- A process receives SIGTERM (not from our cancel) — e.g., the user kills the agent's child directly, or the OS OOM-killer.\n- `cmd.Wait()` returns `*exec.ExitError` with `Signaled() == true`.\n- The orchestrator context was NOT cancelled.\n\nThis produces a \"real\" failure event, which is arguably correct — but the test `TestRunMulti_DoesNotEmitSyntheticRunErrorOnCancellation` (run_multi_test.go around line 794-820 in the PR) only exercises the `context.Canceled` waitErr path. It does not exercise the case where the parser already emitted a `Finished{Success:true}` event and the process then exits with `-1` due to a stray signal — that's the \"torn stdout\" scenario where you'd want both the in-stream Finished AND the synthetic RunError to surface, but the user gets confused output (\"succeeded\" + \"synthetic error\" together).\n\nThe classification in `classifyStatus` correctly demotes to `Failed` in this case (`waitErr != nil` after the `ctx.Err()` check). The concern is the user-visible double-message in the dump. Not a silent failure — but inverse of one: a noisy false-positive.\n\n**Recommendation:** Document the SIGTERM-from-outside-our-context case explicitly in `shouldEmitSyntheticRunError`. Optionally, when `errors.As(waitErr, &exitErr)` and `exitErr.ExitCode() == -1` AND no signal handler is configured, prefer the original waitErr without the synthetic event (it's already classified Failed; the synthetic event adds no information).\n\n---\n\n### 6. MEDIUM — `formatErrorPreview` returns the first non-empty stderr line; pure-ANSI stderr produces an empty preview that masks the failure\n\n**Location:** `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/cmd/entire/cli/review/tui_model.go:430-446` (PR branch, `formatErrorPreview`).\n\n```go\nfunc formatErrorPreview(err error) string {\n    if err == nil { return \"\" }\n    var pe *reviewtypes.ProcessError\n    if errors.As(err, &pe) {\n        for _, line := range strings.Split(pe.Stderr, \"\\n\") {\n            if trimmed := strings.TrimSpace(line); trimmed != \"\" {\n                return trimmed\n            }\n        }\n        if pe.Err != nil {\n            return pe.Err.Error()\n        }\n    }\n    return err.Error()\n}\n```\n\nThe \"first non-empty line\" rule is reasonable for plain text but breaks for stderr that contains only ANSI escape codes (think `codex` and `claude-code` emitting colored failure banners). After `strings.TrimSpace`, an ANSI-only line is non-empty (escape bytes are not whitespace), so the preview shows `\\x1b[31mError\\x1b[0m...` literally — which then runs through `sanitizeDisplayText` + `CollapseWhitespace` at the caller and may collapse to something user-hostile.\n\nMore subtly: if the first line is ANSI-only chrome and the second line is the real error message, the user sees the chrome and not the message.\n\n**Recommendation:** Strip ANSI escapes before picking the first non-empty line:\n```go\nline = stripAnsi(strings.TrimSpace(line))\nif line != \"\" { return line }\n```\n\nThere's already an `ansi` package in use (test file imports `ansi.StringWidth`). Use its strip helper, or `regexp.MustCompile(\"\\x1b\\\\[[0-9;]*m\").ReplaceAllString(line, \"\")`.\n\n**Also:** when `pe.Stderr` is entirely empty AND `pe.Err` is nil (defensive: both fields unset), the function falls through to `err.Error()` — which on a `*ProcessError` produces `\"<agent>: <nil>\"`. That's user-hostile but technically not silent. Worth adding `return \"(no error detail available)\"` as a final fallback.\n\n---\n\n### 7. MEDIUM — `dumpAgent` swallows `mdrender.RenderForWriter` errors twice — once silently per file\n\n**Location:** `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/cmd/entire/cli/review/dump.go:90-95` (current; predates this PR but the PR builds on top of it without addressing).\n\n```go\nrendered, err := mdrender.RenderForWriter(s.W, b.String())\nif err != nil {\n    rendered = b.String()\n}\nfmt.Fprint(s.W, rendered)\n```\n\nThis is a fallback that's reasonable in spirit but doesn't log. If the markdown renderer is broken (a real regression in `mdrender`), every user gets the raw markdown back with no indication something failed. Since this PR doubles down on the dump path as the primary failure-visibility surface, a `logging.Debug` here would help operators triage \"renderer broken\" reports.\n\n**Recommendation:** Add a `logging.Debug` when the fallback fires. Not a Warn — the user still sees content, so this is genuinely a soft fallback.\n\n---\n\n### 8. LOW — `fanIn` channel send in `emitEnrichedAgentTokens` can block indefinitely if the dispatch loop has already exited\n\n**Location:** `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/cmd/entire/cli/review/run_multi.go:213` (PR branch).\n\n```go\nfanIn <- taggedEvent{agentIdx: agentIdx, ev: enriched.Tokens}\n```\n\nThis send happens inside the per-agent forwarding goroutine **after** `p.Wait()` returns. The `wg.Wait()`/`close(fanIn)` orchestration runs in a separate goroutine and is only triggered by `wg.Done`, which only fires when the forwarding goroutine returns. So the send is safe — but a future change that returns early from the forwarding goroutine (e.g., a `select` with `ctx.Done()`) would deadlock here because `close(fanIn)` doesn't happen until `wg.Done()`.\n\nThis is not a current bug, but it's a fragile invariant worth documenting. The `fanIn` channel has buffer `len(reviewers)*16`, but for N=8 agents emitting Started + AssistantText × 10 + Tokens + Finished + synthetic RunError + enriched Tokens, you can easily exceed 128 buffered events. Synchronous blocking sends then depend on the dispatch loop draining at line `for tagged := range fanIn` — which is fine in the normal path.\n\n**Recommendation:** Document the invariant in the function-level comment, or wrap the send with a `select { case fanIn <- ...: case <-ctx.Done(): }` to drop the enrichment on cancellation. Right now if `ctx` is cancelled mid-run, the enrichment can still be queued (and consumed) which is functional but wasteful.\n\n---\n\n### 9. LOW — `enriched` `AgentRun` is constructed but only `Tokens` is read; other fields silently discarded\n\n**Location:** `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/cmd/entire/cli/review/run_multi.go:208-214` (PR branch, `emitEnrichedAgentTokens`).\n\n```go\nenriched := cfg.EnrichAgentRun(ctx, run)\nif enriched.Tokens.In == 0 && enriched.Tokens.Out == 0 {\n    return\n}\nfanIn <- taggedEvent{agentIdx: agentIdx, ev: enriched.Tokens}\n```\n\nThe enricher gets the full `AgentRun` and can return a modified `AgentRun`. But the dispatch loop only consumes the `enriched.Tokens` field via the synthetic `Tokens` event. If an enricher returns `Err` set to something non-nil (e.g., \"transcript truncated; tokens are partial\"), that error is silently dropped. The function name implies enrichment, but the contract is hidden: only `Tokens` enrichment actually flows.\n\n**Recommendation:** Either:\n- Document this restriction on `RunConfig.EnrichAgentRun`: \"only `Tokens` modifications are propagated to live sinks; other field changes are ignored until the summary-level `EnrichSummary` runs.\"\n- Or fan out a richer event type (e.g., a hypothetical `AgentRunUpdate` event) and consume the whole struct.\n\nThe current behavior is fine for the immediate use case but invites future bugs where a maintainer wires up additional fields and wonders why they don't appear.\n\n---\n\n### 10. INFO — `tui_sink.go:83-89` swallows `program.Run()` error with explicit `_ = err`\n\n**Location:** `/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/cmd/entire/cli/review/tui_sink.go:81-90`.\n\n```go\ngo func() {\n    defer close(s.done)\n    if _, err := s.program.Run(); err != nil {\n        // Bubble Tea program errors are non-actionable in a background\n        // goroutine (e.g., terminal resize race on exit). Log nothing —\n        // the run result is available